{
 "research_question": "Does a failed Pick and Roll (PNR) possession lead to a league-wide counter-strategy of using PULL_UP shots, evidenced by a positive lift in points per possession (PPR) for PULL_UP shots in at least 70% of relevant units, with a median lift greater than 2 points per possession?",
 "retrieval_summary": {
  "prior_findings": [
   {
    "id": "CF001",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "The PNR play type is a primary offensive action, and its efficiency is sensitive to defensive scheme and personnel. Initial PNR success or failure heavily influences subsequent offensive choices."
   },
   {
    "id": "CF002",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "PULL_UP shots are a common secondary option when primary actions like PNR are disrupted, particularly for ball-dominant guards. Their efficiency can vary widely based on player skill and defensive pressure."
   },
   {
    "id": "F007",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "The 'PNR Fail' state (defined as a PNR possession yielding <0.8 PPP) often correlates with a subsequent shift to isolation or secondary offensive actions. The specific follow-up actions depend on team strategy and player archetypes."
   }
  ],
  "failed_experiments": [],
  "limitations": [
   {
    "id": "L003",
    "text": "Defining 'PNR fail' consistently across different contexts and play-by-play data is challenging due to the fluid nature of basketball. The initial definition of <0.8 PPP might be too simplistic."
   },
   {
    "id": "L005",
    "text": "Attributing 'lift' to a specific counter-strategy requires careful control for confounding variables such as defensive adjustment, game state, and player fatigue, which are not always fully captured."
   }
  ],
  "unresolved": [
   {
    "id": "UQ001",
    "text": "What are the most common and effective secondary actions after a failed PNR? Is there a hierarchy of counter-strategies?"
   },
   {
    "id": "UQ002",
    "text": "How does defensive scheme (e.g., hedging, dropping, switching) specifically influence the effectiveness of PULL_UP shots following a PNR failure?"
   }
  ]
 },
 "evidence_use": "The retrieved information confirms that PNR is a primary action and PULL_UP shots are a common secondary option. It also supports the concept that PNR failure leads to shifts in offensive strategy (CF001, CF002, F007). The definition of 'PNR fail' (F007, L003) as <0.8 PPP provides a starting point but highlights a potential limitation in its simplicity. The unresolved questions (UQ001, UQ002) directly relate to this research question, indicating it addresses a gap in understanding. The limitations (L003, L005) regarding consistent definition of 'PNR fail' and controlling for confounding variables are critical and must be addressed in the design.",
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.8,
  "blocking_constraints": []
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "CF001",
   "CF002",
   "F007",
   "L003",
   "L005",
   "UQ001",
   "UQ002"
  ],
  "summary": "Prior work has established PNR as a primary offensive action and PULL_UP shots as a common secondary option. It has also shown that PNR failures lead to shifts in offensive strategy. However, the specific efficacy and league-wide prevalence of PULL_UP as a counter-strategy with quantified 'lift' after PNR failure, as posed, remains an open question."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The 'lift' in PULL_UP PPP might be confounded by defensive adjustments, game situation, or player specific skill rather than solely being a direct counter to PNR failure. Player selection bias (only good pull-up shooters attempt them) also confounds the lift."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "Defining 'PNR fail' consistently as <0.8 PPP might not fully capture all instances of a 'failed' PNR, potentially misclassifying possessions or introducing noise. The threshold might be arbitrary and not reflect actual defensive success/failure across all teams."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While PNR and PULL_UPs are common, the specific subset of 'PULL_UPs immediately following a PNR fail' might have insufficient sample size for individual units (teams/players) to reliably detect a 2pp lift, especially for less frequent occurrences."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The 'PNR fail' label is derived from the outcome (PPP < 0.8), which could introduce circularity or bias if not carefully handled in the analysis of subsequent outcomes."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.4,
  "rejected": 0.3,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of a 'PNR fail' (defined as a PNR possession yielding <0.8 PPP) on the subsequent offensive possession's choice of PULL_UP shot and its associated points per possession (PPP) 'lift' for individual units (teams or primary ball-handlers). The estimand is specifically the median lift in PPP for PULL_UP shots in possessions immediately following a PNR fail, across relevant units, and the proportion of units showing a positive lift.",
  "unit": "The unit of analysis will be a 'possession sequence', defined as a PNR possession followed immediately by the next offensive possession. For the 'lift' calculation, the unit will be 'team-seasons' or 'primary ball-handler-seasons' to assess the '70% of units' criterion.",
  "sample": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs. Data will be filtered to include only PNR possessions and the immediately subsequent offensive possession. 'PNR fail' will be defined as PNR possessions yielding <0.8 PPP. PULL_UP shots will be identified from play-by-play data. Only primary ball-handlers involved in sufficient PNR and PULL_UP attempts will be included for individual unit analysis (e.g., minimum 100 PNR-fail sequences and 50 PULL_UP attempts following PNR-fail).",
  "power": "A power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum number of 'PNR fail followed by PULL_UP' sequences required per unit to detect a 2pp lift with 80% power at alpha=0.05, assuming a baseline PULL_UP PPP and variance. If insufficient power for individual units, analysis will aggregate to conference/league level, or the scope of 'unit' will be adjusted.",
  "controls": "Control for game state (score differential, quarter, time remaining), opponent defensive scheme (if available and reliable), and player on-court combinations using fixed effects for player and team. Consider a matched-pair design comparing PULL_UP PPP after PNR fail vs. PULL_UP PPP after successful PNR or other non-PNR offensive initiations, to control for baseline PULL_UP efficiency. Baseline PULL_UP PPP will be established for each unit. The 'lift' will be calculated as PULL_UP PPP after PNR fail minus the unit's baseline PULL_UP PPP in general scenarios.",
  "falsification": "If the median lift in PULL_UP PPP following PNR fail is not greater than 0, or if the proportion of units showing positive lift is significantly less than 70%, the hypothesis is rejected. A falsification test could also involve examining if other secondary actions (e.g., isolation, post-up) show a significantly higher or more prevalent 'lift' after PNR fail.",
  "multiplicity": "Given multiple units (teams/players) will be tested for 'lift > 0', a False Discovery Rate (FDR) control method (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg) will be applied to adjust p-values for the '>=70% of units' criterion. The median lift will be reported as a single league-wide aggregate statistic.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis is supported if: 1) The league-wide median 'lift' in PULL_UP PPP (after PNR fail vs. baseline PULL_UP PPP) is > 2.0pp; AND 2) At least 70% of individual units (team-seasons or primary ball-handler-seasons) show a statistically significant positive 'lift' (p < 0.05 after FDR correction) in PULL_UP PPP following a PNR fail.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete), NBA 2025-26 playoffs (complete). Derived play-type possession records from the graph database."
 }

